1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Most channels lose a third of their viewers in the first thirty seconds.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:09,146
Today I want to show you exactly why that happens.

3
00:00:09,346 --> 00:00:09,946
um

4
00:00:10,146 --> 00:00:13,608
And what you can change before your next upload.

5
00:00:15,208 --> 00:00:16,746
Let's start with titles.

6
00:00:17,046 --> 00:00:22,431
Your title has one job, which is to make a promise the viewer believes.

7
00:00:22,731 --> 00:00:26,962
A title like amazing tips for growth promises nothing at all.

8
00:00:27,262 --> 00:00:31,108
A title with a number in it promises something specific.

9
00:00:31,308 --> 00:00:34,769
I tested forty titles across two channels last spring.

10
00:00:35,069 --> 00:00:39,300
The ones with a concrete figure got clicked twice as often.

11
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,369
so yeah

12
00:00:40,969 --> 00:00:45,969
Keep the title under sixty characters so search does not cut it off.

13
00:00:46,269 --> 00:00:52,038
And put the subject in the first forty, because that is all a phone shows.

14
00:00:53,838 --> 00:00:54,608
Now thumbnails.

15
00:00:54,908 --> 00:00:57,600
The thumbnail should never repeat the title.

16
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:03,285
If the title says the number, the thumbnail shows the face or the result.

17
00:01:03,485 --> 00:01:08,100
Three words is the most a thumbnail can carry at feed size.

18
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:13,015
A fourth word turns into a grey smear on a phone screen.

19
00:01:13,315 --> 00:01:13,915
uh

20
00:01:14,215 --> 00:01:18,062
Test two versions and keep the one that holds attention.

21
00:01:19,762 --> 00:01:23,223
Why do viewers leave your videos at thirty seconds?

22
00:01:23,523 --> 00:01:25,831
It is almost never the topic.

23
00:01:26,131 --> 00:01:31,515
It is the gap between what the title promised and what the opening delivers.

24
00:01:31,815 --> 00:01:37,585
If the first line does not confirm the click, people go back to the feed.

25
00:01:37,885 --> 00:01:39,808
I think the fix is

26
00:01:40,208 --> 00:01:40,808
um

27
00:01:41,008 --> 00:01:46,392
I think the fix is to say the promise again in the first sentence.

28
00:01:46,692 --> 00:01:50,923
Then open a question the viewer cannot close on their own.

29
00:01:51,223 --> 00:01:55,069
And show them the payoff exists before the minute mark.

30
00:01:57,069 --> 00:01:58,223
Finally, retention graphs.

31
00:01:58,523 --> 00:02:02,369
Export the audience retention chart from studio once a week.

32
00:02:02,669 --> 00:02:07,285
Look for cliffs, which are single moments where many people leave together.

33
00:02:07,585 --> 00:02:13,354
A cliff is usually a topic change with no signpost, or a long sponsor read.

34
00:02:13,654 --> 00:02:18,654
The slow slide across the middle is pacing, and the fix is cutting.

35
00:02:18,954 --> 00:02:23,185
Cut the middle by a fifth and watch the slide flatten.

36
00:02:23,485 --> 00:02:27,715
That is the whole system, and it fits on one page.
